    'Revision Exercise 2 Q16(a)-(c)',
    'Look at the circuit diagram in Figure 2.73 (Bulb A in the main series line with a parallel branch containing Bulb B on one side and Bulb C + closed switch on the other). Each light bulb is identical (resistance $R$): (a) Is this a series or parallel circuit? Explain your answer. (b) How do the brightness of bulbs A, B and C compare? (Which is the brightest?) (c) What would happen to the brightness of the bulbs if the switch in series with C was opened? Explain your answer.',
    '(a) It is a combination (series-parallel) circuit because Bulb A is connected in series with the parallel combination of Bulbs B and C; (b) Bulb A is the BRIGHTEST, while Bulbs B and C are equally dim ($A > B = C$), because Bulb A carries the full main current $I$ whereas the current splits equally ($\\frac{I}{2}$) between the identical parallel branches B and C; (c) If the switch is opened, Bulb C goes OUT completely, the total circuit resistance increases from $1.5R$ to $2R$ (reducing the main current from $\\frac{2V}{3R}$ to $\\frac{V}{2R}$), so Bulb A becomes slightly DIMMER while Bulb B becomes BRIGHTER (now carrying the full current equal to Bulb A, so $A = B$)',
