    'Answer the following from Exercise 3.3: (1) How can a student turn an iron nail into a temporary magnet and test it? (2) Student A used the stroking method while Student B placed an identical nail inside a DC solenoid; why did Student B\'s nail pick up more paperclips? (3) Do you agree with a technician who suggests heating a magnet to make it stronger? (4) How can a workshop magnetic lifting tool be designed so rough handling does not easily demagnetise it? (5) What pattern do iron filings form over a bar magnet and what does it show? (6) Compare the field patterns of a bar magnet and a horseshoe magnet. (7) Is magnetic field strength the same all around a bar magnet?',
