    '(1) Heat the nail in a flame; (2) Stroking is always stronger than a solenoid; (3) Agree, heat strengthens magnets; (4) Use pure soft iron and drop it often; (5) Straight lines; (6) Identical patterns; (7) Agree, field strength is uniform everywhere',
    '(1) Rub with silk; (2) AC current was used by B; (3) Heating aligns domains; (4) Store without keepers; (5) Concentric circles; (6) Horseshoe magnets have only one pole; (7) Strongest at the exact centre of the bar',
    '(1) Dip in acid; (2) Nails cannot be magnetised; (3) Curie temperature doubles magnetism; (4) Hammer in E–W direction; (5) No pattern forms; (6) Bar magnets have stronger gap fields; (7) Field strength increases with distance',
    'Step 1: DC solenoid magnetisation is stronger than single-touch stroking. Step 2: Heating demagnetises magnets via thermal agitation. Step 3: Horseshoe magnets concentrate a strong uniform field between closely spaced N and S poles, and field density is greatest at the poles.',
