    'Answer Questions 7 to 10 from Revision Exercise 3: (7) How do you test if a material is definitely a magnet (vs. unmagnetised iron), and how do you determine which end of an unlabelled bar magnet is the North pole? (8) Compare domain arrangements in unmagnetised vs. magnetised iron, and state what happens if a magnet is cut in half. (9) Summarize magnetisation and demagnetisation methods. (10) State the properties of magnetic lines of force and describe magnetic shielding.',
    '(7) REPULSION is the only sure test for a permanent magnet (attraction can occur with any unmagnetised ferromagnetic material); to find the North pole of an unlabelled bar magnet, either suspend it freely by a thread—the end pointing toward Geographic North is the North-seeking pole—or bring a known compass/magnet pole near it (the end that repels a known North pole is the North pole). (8) In unmagnetised iron, domain dipoles point in random directions so their net field is zero; in magnetised iron, domains align parallel in the same direction; cutting a magnet in half produces two smaller complete N–S magnets. (9) Magnetisation: stroking, DC solenoid, induction; Demagnetisation: AC solenoid in E–W, heating, hammering in E–W. (10) Properties of magnetic lines of force: they are continuous closed loops running from North to South outside the magnet (and S to N inside), they never cross each other, they are in a state of longitudinal tension (tend to shorten) and lateral repulsion, and their closeness indicates field strength; Magnetic shielding encloses sensitive instruments inside a high-permeability soft-iron ring/box that diverts external magnetic lines of force through its walls, leaving the interior cavity free of magnetic fields',
