    'Revision Exercise 3 Q11, Q12 & Q13',
    'Answer Questions 11, 12, and 13 from Revision Exercise 3: (11) Suppose you mount a bar magnet on rubber wheels (Figure 3.56, Front = N, Rear = S) and place the "magnetic car" on the magnetic N–S line pointing geographic north: will it roll towards the north, towards the south, or remain stationary? Explain. (12) Can 4 strong bar magnets be arranged in stable square and cross configurations (Figure 3.57)? How must their poles be oriented? (13) You are given two identical-looking iron bars A and B (Figure 3.58), one of which is a strong permanent magnet and the other is unmagnetised iron; using only the two bars, how can you determine which bar is the magnet?',
    '(11) The magnetic car will NOT roll (it remains stationary) because the Earth\'s magnetic field is uniform over the small length of the bar magnet: the northward pull on its North pole ($+mB_H$) is exactly balanced by the equal and opposite southward pull on its South pole ($-mB_H$), giving zero net translational force. (12) Yes, the square configuration is stable when the 4 magnets are arranged head-to-tail around the perimeter ($\\text{N–S} \\rightarrow \\text{N–S} \\rightarrow \\text{N–S} \\rightarrow \\text{N–S}$) so unlike poles meet at every corner; for a cross configuration, alternating poles or external restraint is required because four identical poles meeting at the centre repel, whereas two N and two S alternating around the central junction attract adjacent neighbours. (13) Touch the end of Bar A to the exact MIDDLE of Bar B (forming a "T" shape): because a magnet has almost zero magnetic field at its centre, if there is strong attraction, Bar A is the permanent magnet; if there is no attraction when A\'s end touches B\'s middle (and strong attraction when B\'s end touches A\'s middle), then Bar B is the permanent magnet',
